## Telemetry.dev separates cached-token reporting before calculating costs

Source: telemetry.dev — https://telemetry.dev/blog/llm-cost-monitoring-account-for-cached-tokens
Published: 2026-11-14
Channel: news

Telemetry.dev is an AI observability service that traces model calls, tool operations and retrievals using OpenTelemetry, a standard for collecting telemetry. Its cost-monitoring guide explains why prompt caching can make token dashboards disagree with invoices: providers report cached input using different conventions, so a shared summing rule can count tokens twice or miss them.

The guide first maps raw usage into uncached input, cache reads and cache writes. It describes OpenAI and Gemini cache hits as included in input totals, while Anthropic reports cache activity separately. Each bucket then receives the applicable model rate, with output priced separately. The article cautions that explicit Gemini caches also incur storage costs outside per-call token fields.

Next, retain raw usage beside normalized counts on each span, the record of a call, so calculations can be corrected later. Mark missing usage as unknown rather than zero. Finally, compare a known window of priced calls with the matching invoice. Cache hit rate alone establishes neither savings nor the full bill; rates and reporting fields still need checking for the model in use.

Why it matters: For engineers maintaining model-cost dashboards: validate provider-specific normalization against invoices before treating cache hit rate as a spending result.

