## LangChain groups agent work around tasks people can review together

Source: LangChain — https://www.langchain.com/blog/goal-engineering
Published: 2026-11-14
Channel: news

Actively AI builds agents for sales teams. In a LangChain article coauthored with its researchers, the company describes changing how those agents divide their work so salespeople can review outputs and provide useful feedback more easily. The focus is human evaluation of subjective work, rather than a new tracing feature.

The original design assigned an agent to each prospective customer account. Each agent handled several kinds of work, and reviewable actions arrived at different times. The authors report that this forced salespeople to switch between agents and made it awkward to share lessons about doing the same job across accounts.

The replacement groups agents into motions: recurring jobs such as outreach or early deal progression. First, similar tasks sit together for review. Then feedback about a seller’s preferences can inform other tasks within that motion. Background agents still gather account information, which the working agents retrieve as needed. The authors argue that aligning agent scope with review makes feedback easier to collect. This is a qualitative practitioner account, without a controlled performance comparison.

Why it matters: For teams designing human evaluation workflows: examine whether reviewers can assess similar agent actions together and reuse the resulting feedback.

